  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Rain with isolated thunderstorms today. Dense \nfog possible Downeast this morning. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\nIntensifying low pressure moves northeast across Quebec \nProvince, along a frontal boundary, today. A pre-frontal trof \nwill cross the region later today through early tonight. The \nlow then tracks north of Maine tonight drawing the cold front \nacross the region overnight through early Tuesday. Rain will \ndevelop across the region today, then persist through early \ntonight. Although instability will be limited, could also have \nisolated thunderstorms today. The rain will taper to showers in\nthe wake of the pre-frontal trof later tonight. Could still \nhave a slight chance/chance of showers across mostly northern \nareas early Tuesday with the cold front and exiting upper low. \nModels generally agree that the greater rain totals with this \nevent should occur along the track of the low across Quebec \nprovince. The greatest rain totals across the forecast area \nshould occur across northwest areas, nearest to the low track, \nwhere around an inch or more of rain is possible. However, \nuncertainty still exists regarding rainfall totals across the \nremainder of the forecast area dependent on the location and \npersistence of where areas of heavier rain might develop. Areas \nof dense fog are also possible this morning with moisture moving\ninland from the Gulf of Maine. The best chance for dense fog \nwill occur across Downeast areas.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Scattered showers and thunderstorms late this \nweek. Some may be severe.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\nHigh pressure over the area mid week weakens Thursday as upper\nlevel shortwave energy approaches from the west. Models are \ncurrently showing the potential for weak low pressure \ndevelopment in the area which may kick off some showers and \nstorms along and ahead of a cold front expected to move through\nthe area sometime Friday. While relatively far out and despite \ndifferences in timing and intensity of the cold front and\nupper-level energy, long range model soundings show a favorable\nstorm environment with very warm surface temperatures \ncontributing to somewhere between 1000 and 1500 J/kg of \ninstability with around 30 kts of effective bulk shear. This may\npose a risk for some severe storms. Additionally, global \nensembles are showing total atmospheric moisture in the 95th \npercentile which in combination with high freezing levels and \ntall, skinny instability profiles may favor some heavy rainfall.",
